<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Megasthenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–290 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Megasthenes was a Greek historian and diplomat sent by the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> as envoy to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> around 300 BCE. His Indica, known only from quotations by later writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Diodorus, described Indian geography, cities, peoples and a society of seven classes, and was the main Western source on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Little is known about Megasthenes as a person. He spent time at the court of Sibyrtius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a> under Antigonus I and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>. Arrian writes that Megasthenes lived with Sibyrtius and often spoke of visiting Sandracottus, the king of the Indians, that is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>. He served as ambassador of the Seleucid king Seleucus I Nicator at Chandragupta's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>.</p><p>The date of the journey is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> reigned from 305 to 281 BCE, which gives the loose range within which the mission might have begun. Scholars dispute when he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and how long he stayed: A. B. Bosworth argued for a date before Seleucus, while Stoneman and others argue for a date after the Mauryan-Seleucid settlement of about 303 BCE. He appears to have passed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, since he gives a detailed account of its rivers, and then travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the Ganga. It is not certain which other parts of India he saw. Later Greek envoys are known: Deimachus went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara</a> and Dionysius to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Megasthenes described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in his book Indica, the first written description of India by a person from the Western world. The work is lost and survives in part through quotations by later authors.</p><p>Ancient opinion was divided. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> was the only ancient writer to speak favourably of him, and Diodorus quotes him while omitting some parts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> accused him of falsehood, though he borrowed much of his content on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called him a liar for fabulous stories and wrote that no faith whatever could be placed in Deimachus and Megasthenes. Pliny the Elder criticised his description of the fabulous races of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and his account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Herakles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>. Modern scholars such as E. A. Schwanbeck and B. C. J. Timmer have found him a generally reliable source of Indian history. Truesdell Sparhawk Brown is more critical, noting that he saw only a small part of India and relied on others, but his work remained the principal source for later writers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Megasthenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In a war and treaty with Seleucus I Nicator around 305–303 BCE, he gained Arachosia, Gedrosia and the Paropamisadae, covering much of modern Pakistan and parts of Afghanistan, in return for 500 war elephants, and received the Greek envoy <b>Megasthenes</b> at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Megasthenes</b> and Pliny the Elder wrote of the Pandya kingdom, Pliny of its trade with Rome.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Buddhist texts put the Kshatriyas first and described society as householders, and the Greek envoy <b>Megasthenes</b>, around 300 BCE, counted seven hereditary classes.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Megasthenes</b> heard of them around 300 BCE, and Ashoka's edicts in the 3rd century BCE name the Pandyas as independent neighbours beyond his empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-pandyas">Early Pandyas</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 300 BCE the Greek envoy <b>Megasthenes</b> described the Sourasenoi as honouring Herakles, usually taken to mean Krishna, at their cities Methora and Cleisobora.</blockquote><a href="/state/surasena">Surasena</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Asia Minor, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39,34&amp;year=-311" aria-label="Asia Minor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5392 765 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Megasthenes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5842 1035h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39,34&amp;year=-311">What was here in 311 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
